Decrease-and-conquer
Insertion sort pseudocode
function insertionSort(arr):
	n  length(arr)		// Get number of elements in arr
	for i from 1 to n-1:	// Start from second element
		key = arr[i]	// Store the current element
		j  i – 1		// Initialize position for comparison
		// Move elements of arr[0..i-1] that are greater than key to one position ahead
		while j > 0 and arr[j] < key:	// Second comparison keeps sort stable, not =>
			arr[j + 1]  arr[j]	// Shift elements to the right
			j  j – 1			// Move to next element to the left
		arr[j + 1 ]  key		// Insert the key into its correct position
	end for
end function

Algorithm inserts checking from the right to the left into the sorted part of the array.
Time Complexity
Best case: O(n)	-Just one time through the array, occurs when array is already sorted
Worst case: O(n2)	-Occurs when the array is sorted in reverse order, maximum number 
 			  of times through the for and while loop
Average case: O(n2)	-Each element requires shifting about half of previous elements, and 
 			  the inner loop runs about halfway through the sorted portion
Space Complexity
O(1)			-Constant space complexity, requires a single additional variable

Insertion sort is stable due to the inner loop checking if arr[j] > key, not equal to the key. Since it is shifting from the right, a key that is equal to j causes the loop to end and the key is inserted to the right of the element at j, keeping the relative order of elements that are equal.
