Transform-and-conquer
Heapsort pseudocode
//Uses 0-based indexing
function heapify(arr, n, i):
	largest  i
	left  2*i + 1
	right  2*i + 2
	if left < n and arr[left] > arr[largest] then
		largest  left
	end if
	if right < n and arr[right] < arr[largest] then
		largest  right
	end if
	if largest ≠ i then
		swap(arr[i], arr[largest]
		heapify(arr, n, largest)
	end if
end function
function heapsort(arr):
	n  length(arr)
	//Build max heap
	for i  floor(n/2) - 1 downto 0 do
		heapify(arr, n, i)
	end for
	// Extract elements one by one
	for i  n - 1 downto 0 do
		swap(arr[0], arr[i])
		heapify(arr, i, 0)
	end for
end function
Heapsort is not stable, so additional work must be done to reorder equal elements into the correct relative order. Heapsort will freely swap equal elements while building and restoring the heap. Comparing and sorting equal elements according to their initial position in the data will overcome this obstacle to stability.
Best/Worst/Average Case: O(n log n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

Ordinary heapsort will most likely return an order that is not stable. My fix was to implement tie-breaking using the original index where I store each record as:
(id, delay, original_index)
Whenever a tie occurs, the original index is used to place the tying elements in ascending order.

