Conclusions:
 	Instrumentation seriously slowed down everything by a magnitude of 10 at least. Counting sort performed exceptionally well (which is to be expected because the key range is quite small comparatively). Since the key range is known to be small, it is the fastest and most efficient algorithm to use in the case where certain characteristics of the data set is known beforehand.
 	In the case where key range is known to be quite large, merge sort or heap sort are better equipped to handle the dataset. Heap sort does take more memory and performed worse than merge, so merge is the all-around best choice of the four if the characteristics of the data set are unknown beforehand.
